this morning around 100 pro-democracy  protesters are barricaded inside a Hong  Kong University in a tense standoff with  police they're facing desperate  conditions after fighting police  yesterday with Molotov cocktails  catapults and bows and arrows  ramie Inocencio is just outside that  university so Raymie  good morning as nightfall comes what's  the situation there good morning yes we  are just outside the campus of  Polytechnic University right behind me  here and just trying to figure out  what's been happening right now we're  trying to know how many people are  inside it's estimated maybe 100 to 200  protesters are there but it really is  tough to know because the several  buildings are basically a labyrinth of  rooms and corridors and quarters that  people can try to hide if police did  really try to raid the campus so it  really is impossible to know the mangled  barricaded main entrance to Hong Kong  Polytechnic University is anything but  welcoming and campus life is littered  with the debris of conflict in the  makeshift infirmary volunteer medics  tells CBS News they've treated more than  100 injuries the worst a collapsed lung  from an officer's rubber bullet food  supplies are dwindling but cooks in the  canteen keep frying up rice to feed the  last of the resistance this is the heart  of the University's campus defences  right in front of me are hundreds of  Molotov cocktails here the smell of the  flammable material is very much in your  face as the violence intensified Monday  many protesters tried to escape in  different ways descending daringly over  bridges are dropping into dirty sewers  but Tuesday afternoon many protestors  cold hungry and wrapped in thermal  blankets surrendered and walked off  campus still others chose to stay like  John and Jackie how are you feeling  right now after three days being  here very down do you feel that you've  lost this university is not the last  last battle yeah you can say just is  another battlefield now anyone under 18  was let go but anyone 18 and older was  immediately arrested by police earlier  at a press conference chief executive  Carrie Lam said at least 400 adults have  been arrested so far they could be  detained and charged but if they are not  charged they could be set free but right  now it is just any anyone's guess as to  what could happen moving ahead  Gail I'll say six months and Counting  still no sign of a solution there thank  you very much ray me  innocencio reporting for us from Hong  Kong
 

